@1Tyvis A QB’s success in the NFL is 75% mental and 25% physical. Every level of football prior to the NFL, those figures are flipped.  

It’s really hard for a guy who’s been a football savant his entire life to accept that he now needs to study really hard. Some never make it past this point. The ones who do, then must realize they don’t actually know how to study bc it’s always come so easy to them. So concurrent with studying, they also need to figure out how to study in a way that works for them—usually by trial and error. You’ve got to figure out those two enormous things, then actually consume all of the study materials, retain the information, and apply it on the field, while dealing with the physical rigors of the NFL. This all has to be done in a very short period of time, while remaining healthy and managing to produce enough on the field to hold onto or earn a starting job. Further exacerbating all of this, is the best QB prospects typically end up in the most dysfunctional franchises due to the draft structure. This is why historically, QBs sat and learned for many years before taking over the starting job. But this only works now if you draft a QB when you already have a very good starter. The patience for that system has mostly been lost. Then you have franchises who haven’t had a good QB in decades—so they couldn’t mentor a young QB in this way even if they wanted to. 

It’s one of the hardest jobs in the world.

